          "start_ms": null,
          "status": "needs-review",
          "text": "Description: On October 4, 1954, between 6:40 and 7:00 p.m., a 10-year-old girl (T1) riding her bicycle saw at a distance of 20 meters an \"object\" she initially mistook for a camping tent. According to the child, the oval-shaped object was 2 meters wide and 2.5-3 meters tall, with a conical top, and rested on a rectangular base. As she approached, a hatch (1 x 0.6 meters) opened. A man leapt from the object, closed the hatch behind him, and stood motionless. Of normal size, he had a \"square-shaped\" head, piercing eyes, a long brown-red fur-covered garment. As the man advanced, the frightened child ran away without looking back. Two other individuals reported at the same time and in the direction where the girl was located, one seeing \"a very bright red light vehicle\" that he didn't pay attention to, the other hearing a cream-like noise that made her leave her home. The child's father and a neighbor went to the observation site the same day at 9:00 p.m. and noted that the grass was \"slightly crushed in a circular manner.\" The gendarmerie visited the site on October 6 but was unable to make any observations. No further details were gathered during the investigation. In summary, for this case, there are four distinct facts:\n\n- A single witness who saw an object and \"a human being\" near her path. She did not see the object take off.\n\n- Simultaneously, a farmer saw in the sky a very bright red light vehicle.\n\n- A third person heard a long noise similar to that of a cream-making machine.\n\n- The child's father and a farmer found at 9:00 p.m. the same evening, at the observation site of T1, grass crushed in a circular manner.\n\nNo conclusive evidence links these four facts. Assuming they are linked, one possible hypothesis is:\n\n- Several elements seem compatible with a helicopter, perhaps a Bell 47, seen from the front (oval shape): the rotor mast, the hatch, the noise, the crushed grass, the pilot. The location (\"on a grassy path leading to a small clearing\") could be suitable for a landing. However, the rectangular base, the position of the hatch (would the witness have noted its width if it had been on the side?), and the bright red light of the object are less easily interpreted. This case appears of average strangeness as a simple hypothesis can more or less fit. Its consistency is weak, based mainly on a single witness (the child) and a brief observation.\n\nThe GEIPAN classifies this case as C due to lack of information and cross-checking.",
          "translation_text_sha256": "fdf00d0b66f5665165a5cc3cea0c12c1b50e9e546553737b60c62c9a9b339fb7"
        }
